From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: the key store and the watchdog, the two that panic if absent

Two of the nine Sweet* drivers, and the reason they are next rather than the
ones that look more interesting: these are the two whose absence is not a
missing feature but a missing service that a daemon treats as fatal.

  - **watchdogd** is a launchd job with `_PanicOnCrash`. It opens
    IOServiceMatching("IOWatchdog") with type 1 and, if the service is not
    there, exits - and its exit is a panic. It then looks up the IOService
    carrying `IOPMUPrimary = 1` to write the boot stage to the PMU, and
    crashes if that is missing too (customer builds ignore -wdt_no_pmu). So a
    board with no IOWatchdog does not "lack watchdog support", it panics
    shortly after userspace starts, for a reason that has nothing to do with
    the actual fault.

  - **keybagd** opens IOServiceMatching("AppleKeyStore") with type 0 from
    libaks. `keybagd --init` asks for the system bag (selector 14) and only
    falls back to loading systembag.kb when the answer is kIOReturnNotFound,
    so answering with a handle is the path a userspace reboot takes and no bag
    file is needed.

Both are enabled here because both are **address-free**. That is the whole
selection criterion: every other driver in the set hardcodes three or more
SoC addresses that do not exist on SM6375 (`sweet_spmi.c` 3, `sweet_rpmh.c` 3,
`sweet_usb.c` 3, `sweet_touch.c` 3), and `tools/fdt-find.py` confirms the
blocks are not in this unit's device tree at any base. These two touch no
hardware at all: SweetKeyStore is a selector switch table and a DER encoder,
SweetWatchdog accepts every call and never arms anything. There is no value to
read off the DTB, so there is nothing to get wrong.

What they are, honestly stated:

  - `IOWatchdog` accepts check-in (1), the enable/disable userspace-monitoring
    pair (3/4 and 0/5) and the two struct selectors (2, 9), zeroes the output,
    logs each selector the first time it is seen, and never fires. It carries
    `IOPMUPrimary = 1` and accepts any property write, which is what keeps
    watchdogd's second lookup from crashing.
  - `AppleKeyStore` reports the system bag as handle 0 and the lock state as
    "unlocked since boot", which is the truth for a device with no passcode.
    Unknown selectors return kIOReturnUnsupported and are logged, so the next
    selector the system needs shows up on the console instead of failing
    silently.

Two personalities go into the milanf arm of iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp,
which is where the real link between a driver and the system is: a class that
compiles but has no personality is never instantiated, and both daemons would
fail exactly as if the driver were still an empty translation unit. That is
why the table edit is in this patch and not a separate one.

Both files define the Apple-named classes (`AppleKeyStore`,
`IOWatchdog`) rather than Sweet-prefixed ones, because those are the names the
daemons match on. Checked for collisions: neither class name is defined
anywhere else in the tree, so there is no duplicate-symbol risk and no second
provider to arbitrate with.

IOLog strings keep their SWEET prefix, same as 0088-0090: the pull and
symbolication tooling greps for them.

Verified without a compiler: apply --check exit 0 alone and in series order
behind 0086-0090, then a real apply to a scratch tree - three files cleanly,
CR=0 in sources and patch. Link unverified; CI in the morning.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp
index d11fe26..2224e85 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@
  */
 #include <pexpert/arm64/board_config.h>
 
-#if defined(SWEET)
+#if defined(SWEET) || defined(MILANF)
 #include <IOKit/IOService.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOUserClient.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOKitKeys.h>
@@ -238,4 +238,4 @@ AppleKeyStoreUserClient::externalMethod(uint32_t selector, IOExternalMethodArgum
 	    arg0, in_scalars, in_struct, out_scalars, out_struct, ret);
 	return ret;
 }
-#endif /* SWEET */
+#endif /* SWEET || MILANF */
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetWatchdog.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetWatchdog.cpp
index 8f53742..9f37341 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetWatchdog.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetWatchdog.cpp
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@
  */
 #include <pexpert/arm64/board_config.h>
 
-#if defined(SWEET)
+#if defined(SWEET) || defined(MILANF)
 #include <IOKit/IOService.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOUserClient.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOKitKeys.h>
@@ -104,4 +104,4 @@ IOWatchdogUserClient::externalMethod(uint32_t selector, IOExternalMethodArgument
 	}
 	return kIOReturnSuccess;
 }
-#endif /* SWEET */
+#endif /* SWEET || MILANF */
diff --git a/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp b/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
index 14dbfb6..813de74 100644
--- a/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
+++ b/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
@@ -84,8 +84,9 @@ const char * gIOKernelConfigTables =
     "   },"
 #elif defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
     /*
-     * milanf gets the platform expert and nothing else, for now. Every
-     * other Sweet* driver is still an empty translation unit on this
+     * milanf gets the platform expert plus the two services a device with
+     * no passcode and no reset path needs: AppleKeyStore and IOWatchdog.
+     * Every other Sweet* driver is still an empty translation unit on this
      * board, and a personality whose class does not exist only earns
      * an IOKit "can't find class" line. They come back one at a time,
      * each with its own board values and its own personality here.
@@ -103,6 +104,16 @@ const char * gIOKernelConfigTables =
     "     'IONameMatch'     = 'xiaomi,sweet';"
     "     'IOProbeScore'    = 10000:32;"
     "   },"
+    "   {"
+    "     'IOClass'         = AppleKeyStore;"
+    "     'IOProviderClass' = IOResources;"
+    "     'IOMatchCategory' = AppleKeyStore;"
+    "   },"
+    "   {"
+    "     'IOClass'         = IOWatchdog;"
+    "     'IOProviderClass' = IOResources;"
+    "     'IOMatchCategory' = IOWatchdog;"
+    "   },"
 #endif
     "   {"
     "     'IOClass'         = IOPanicPlatform;"
